{:up ["ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN is_machine_user INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0"
      "ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN for_user_id INTEGER REFERENCES users(id)"
      "ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN password_set_at DATETIME"
      "CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_users_single_machine_user
         ON users (is_machine_user) WHERE is_machine_user = 1"]
 ;; The row goes first, while the flag that identifies it still exists. Dropping
 ;; only the columns would leave `machine-user` behind as an ordinary human row,
 ;; still holding its password — and a later re-migrate would then read it as a
 ;; second *human* user, so its credential would mint an admin token. The one
 ;; thing this migration made possible has to go away with it.
 :down ["DELETE FROM users WHERE is_machine_user = 1"
        "DROP INDEX IF EXISTS idx_users_single_machine_user"
        "ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN password_set_at"
        "ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN for_user_id"
        "ALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN is_machine_user"]
 ;; There is deliberately no `:transactions false` here. Three of the four `:up`
 ;; statements are `ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN`, which SQLite has no `IF NOT EXISTS`
 ;; form of, so a *partial* `:up` cannot be retried: ragtime would not have
 ;; recorded the migration, and the next startup would run statement one against a
 ;; column that already exists — `duplicate column name: is_machine_user`, thrown
 ;; out of `db/init-conn` before anything else can happen, on every boot from then
 ;; on, taking `-main` and `build-app` with it. SQLite has transactional DDL and
 ;; all of these statements are transaction-safe, so leaving transactions on is
 ;; precisely what makes a crash between two of them recoverable. The flag was
 ;; copied from tracker's 044-add-machine-users.edn, which had its own reason;
 ;; cookbook never did.
 }
